---
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import Header from "../../components/Header";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer";

const title = "How to Increase Your Typing Speed: A Complete WPM Improvement Guide";
const description = "A step-by-step guide to building real typing speed without sacrificing accuracy, using deliberate practice, correct form, and the right test settings.";
const canonicalPath = "/blog/how-to-increase-typing-speed/";

const structuredData = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BlogPosting",
    headline: title,
    description,
    datePublished: "2026-09-10",
    dateModified: "2026-09-10",
    author: { "@type": "Organization", name: "FreeTypingTestGoat" },
    publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "FreeTypingTestGoat" },
    mainEntityOfPage: `https://typeittestit.com${canonicalPath}`,
  },
];
---
<SiteLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath={canonicalPath} structuredData={structuredData}>
  <div class="relative min-h-screen">
    <Header client:load />
    <main>
      <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
        <p class="font-label text-accent">Guide</p>
        <h1 class="mt-3 font-heading-3 text-ink">How to Increase Your Typing Speed: A Complete WPM Improvement Guide</h1>
        <p class="mt-6 font-body text-text-muted">Typing faster isn't about frantically mashing keys \u2014 it's a skill you build the same way you'd build any other motor skill: with deliberate, accuracy-first practice. Here's a practical path from wherever you are now to a noticeably higher words-per-minute (WPM) score.</p>

        <h2 class="mt-10 font-heading-4 text-ink">1. Fix your accuracy before chasing speed</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 font-body text-text-muted">Every corrected mistake costs you far more time than typing that letter carefully the first time. If your accuracy is below roughly 95%, slow down. Speed that comes from guessing at keys and fixing errors afterward is slower, in real terms, than typing at a controlled pace with fewer mistakes. Use FreeTypingTestGoat's Easy difficulty and a 1 or 2 minute test to rebuild accuracy before pushing your WPM.</p>

        <h2 class="mt-10 font-heading-4 text-ink">2. Learn (or relearn) proper finger placement</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 font-body text-text-muted">Touch typing relies on a consistent home row position (ASDF for your left hand, JKL; for your right) so each finger is responsible for a fixed set of keys. If you're still looking at the keyboard, this is the highest-leverage fix available: it removes the constant visual search that caps most beginners well below 40 WPM.</p>

        <h2 class="mt-10 font-heading-4 text-ink">3. Practice with purpose, not just volume</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 font-body text-text-muted">Typing the same easy sentence a hundred times trains very little. Instead, vary your practice: use FreeTypingTestGoat's Medium and Hard difficulty levels to expose yourself to less predictable letter combinations, and use longer 3 or 5 minute sessions periodically to build endurance and consistency rather than short bursts of peak speed.</p>

        <h2 class="mt-10 font-heading-4 text-ink">4. Set up your hands and keyboard correctly</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 font-body text-text-muted">Small ergonomic issues quietly slow everyone down. Keep your wrists roughly level with the keyboard (not bent up or down), keep your screen at eye level so you're not craning your neck, and take short breaks every 20\u201330 minutes. Speed built on a tense, uncomfortable setup rarely lasts.</p>

        <h2 class="mt-10 font-heading-4 text-ink">5. Target your specific weak spots</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 font-body text-text-muted">Most typists have a small set of letter pairs or symbols that repeatedly slow them down \u2014 often ones that require a stretch to the number row, or awkward finger transitions like "ed" or "tion". Pay attention to where you hesitate or make repeated mistakes across a few tests, and spend extra practice time specifically on those patterns instead of only repeating full passages.</p>

        <h2 class="mt-10 font-heading-4 text-ink">6. Track your progress instead of guessing</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 font-body text-text-muted">Improvement is easy to miss without numbers. Take a FreeTypingTestGoat test on the same difficulty and duration every few days and watch your WPM, accuracy, and error breakdown over time. Small, steady gains of a few WPM per week compound quickly into a real, lasting improvement.</p>

        <h2 class="mt-10 font-heading-4 text-ink">7. Set a realistic timeline</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 font-body text-text-muted">Most people typing by sight (hunt-and-peck) sit around 20\u201330 WPM. With a few weeks of consistent, accuracy-first touch-typing practice, 40\u201360 WPM is a realistic milestone. Reaching 70+ WPM generally takes sustained practice over months, and the 100+ WPM tier is an elite level reached only with significant deliberate effort. Progress isn't linear \u2014 expect plateaus, and push through them with varied practice rather than just repetition.</p>

        <div class="mt-12 rounded-[24px] border border-hairline bg-canvas p-6 text-center shadow-sm sm:p-8">
          <p class="font-heading-4 text-ink">Ready to put this into practice?</p>
          <p class="mt-2 font-body text-text-muted">Take a free typing test and see exactly where your speed and accuracy stand today.</p>
          <a href="/" class="mt-4 inline-block rounded-full bg-accent px-6 py-3 font-link text-canvas">Start a typing test</a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </main>
    <Footer />
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
